    "q-83705": {
      "dom_id": "step-q83705",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ a & 0 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}c & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ a & 0 & b\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal: \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=-1,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=3.$",
