    "q-37839": {
      "dom_id": "step-q37839",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$\n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 7-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -5 \\\\ 1 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )+5) & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+4) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the quadratic polynomial $\\,{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\,$ we have that the discriminant $D<0.$ So, it doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable over $R.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
