    "q-37845": {
      "dom_id": "step-q37845",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable.  \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible matrix diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-37845-a",
