    "q-37850": {
      "dom_id": "step-q37850",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}15 & -4 & -16 \\\\ 40 & -5 & -40 \\\\ -10 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & ∗ & 0 \\\\ 4 & ∗ & -4 \\\\ -1 & ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the second column of  the matrix $P.$",
      "worked_solution": "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=15,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ \n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{2}I & =[\\begin{aligned}15 & -4 & -16 \\\\ 40 & -5 & -40 \\\\ -10 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 & -16 \\\\ 40 & -4 & -40 \\\\ -10 & 1 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 & -16 & 0 \\\\ 40 & -4 & -40 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 1 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{16}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{4} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 40 & -4 & -40 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-40{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{4} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
