    "q-37849": {
      "dom_id": "step-q37849",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 16 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 20 & 6 \\\\ -12 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}14 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & a & 0 \\\\ 2 & b & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the value of $2a-b.$",
      "worked_solution": "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=14,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=8.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{2}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 16 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 20 & 6 \\\\ -12 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 16 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 22 & 6 \\\\ -12 & 4 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 16 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 22 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 4 & 12 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 22 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -40 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+6{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd ones). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $2a-b=2\\cdot 1-0=2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
