    "q-37250": {
      "dom_id": "step-q37250",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & 1 & 1 \\\\ ∗ & 0 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the first column of the matrix $P.$",
      "worked_solution": "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$\n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
