    "q-37235": {
      "dom_id": "step-q37235",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}23 & -19 & 13 \\\\ 50 & -43 & 32 \\\\ 30 & -27 & 22\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & a & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & b & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the value of $a+b.$",
      "worked_solution": "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ \n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}23 & -19 & 13 \\\\ 50 & -43 & 32 \\\\ 30 & -27 & 22\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}25 & -19 & 13 \\\\ 50 & -41 & 32 \\\\ 30 & -27 & 24\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}25 & -19 & 13 & 0 \\\\ 50 & -41 & 32 & 0 \\\\ 30 & -27 & 24 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{6}{5}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}25 & -19 & 13 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{21}{5} & \\frac{42}{5} & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{7}{5}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}25 & -19 & 13 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$  Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$ \nHence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 2 \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+b=1+1=2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
